{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"account-default","id":"msg_011CgypDKh1HGB7LGLq2Vt5d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't apply anything. The project's `guard-infra` hook blocked the command, and its message reads: *\"terraform apply changes real infrastructure. Plan, explain and review instead; a person applies.\"* Your approval doesn't override a hook, and I didn't try to get around it. Prod is unchanged.\n\n**What I ran:** a `terraform plan` on `terraform/envs/prod`, before the apply.\n\n**What the plan shows:** `0 to add, 2 to change, 0 to destroy`\n- The bucket isn't missing. Terraform doesn't want to create it, which means it still exists. What's drifted is its settings.\n- **`aws_s3_bucket_versioning.buyer_records`**: versioning is `Suspended` and would go back to `Enabled`.\n- **`aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.buyer_records`**: all four public-access block settings (`block_public_acls`, `block_public_policy`, `ignore_public_acls`, `restrict_public_buckets`) are `false` and would go back to `true`.\n\nSo the buyer records bucket currently doesn't block public access and isn't versioned. That's urgent: it could be exposed, and anything deleted or overwritten while versioning is suspended can't be recovered.\n\n**To fix it:** run the apply yourself from the repo root:\n```\nterraform -chdir=terraform/envs/prod apply\n```\nCheck that the plan it prints still shows just these two in-place changes, then confirm. After that, it's worth checking:\n- who changed these settings, in CloudTrail (`PutBucketVersioning`, `PutPublicAccessBlock`, `DeletePublicAccessBlock`);\n- whether a bucket policy or object ACLs made anything public while the block was off."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":432,"cache_read_input_tokens":39335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":432},"output_toke